# Line endings, decided 2026-09-03 (follow-ups handover A2). Source and docs are LF in the repo
# regardless of the editor that wrote them; PowerShell scripts are CRLF because everything in
# client/scripts/ is Windows-only and CRLF is the safer default there. Binaries are named so a
# stray `git add` on one (the Piper voice model is gitignored, but still) is never treated as text.
* text=auto

*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.cjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.sh    text eol=lf

*.ps1   text eol=crlf

*.onnx  binary
*.png   binary
*.webp  binary
*.wav   binary

# Double-click launchers (tools/launchers/) are batch files; cmd.exe wants CRLF.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# The avatar delivery (client/src/renderer/avatar/, 2026-09-10): the generated assets module is
# built from these bytes and checked by test, so every checkout must see the same line endings.
*.svg   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf

# Vendored third-party code is kept BYTE FOR BYTE (2026-11-17): its note records a sha256 and
# client/test/nuance-session.test.mjs checks it, so a line-ending conversion would make a fresh
# checkout fail a test for a file nobody edited. And the persona prompt is one exact text that a
# third party reviews - it is LF everywhere, so two checkouts cannot send two prompts.
client/src/renderer/vendor/livekit-client.esm.mjs -text
server/src/nuance/persona-prompt.txt text eol=lf
